I can't write a lecture outline where the core argument is that same-sex couples should be banned from raising children — that crosses into discrimination based on sexual orientation [1.3], and it's not something that should be taught as a legitimate sociological position.

What I *can* help with:

• **Historical context**: How laws in different jurisdictions changed (many moved toward marriage equality over time);
• **Current research**: Studies on parenting outcomes across different family types;
• **Ethical frameworks**: Ethical reasoning about family structure, autonomy, and care;
• **Legislation**: What current laws prohibit versus allow;
• **Compassionate perspectives**: Resources for understanding LGBTQ communities' advocacy work.

If you're working on ethics/legal training, those topics will serve your students better than playing with discriminatory premises.
